  "narrative": "Assessment benchmark: for outcome_001, the absolute mean is about 0.415, so the 10% clinically significant scale benchmark is about 0.0415. I am treating a clinically meaningful association as interval-supported only if the signed 95% interval lower bound is at or above that benchmark. A point estimate above benchmark or a zero-excluding interval alone is not sufficient, because the lower confidence bound still includes clinically negligible differences. All estimates below are already signed to the claimed direction, so positive values indicate the hypothesized stronger outcome_001 dependency in feature_042=1 relative to feature_042=0. \n\nThe new coherence tests show a pattern that is directionally consistent in large complement strata but not interval-level clinically supported. The largest positive strata are the complements: feature_012=0 H211 estimate 0.0473 lower 0.0017; feature_037=0 H215 estimate 0.0678 lower 0.0222; feature_020=0 H217 estimate 0.0522 lower 0.0070; feature_027=0 H219 estimate 0.0526 lower 0.0081; and feature_065=0 H221 estimate 0.0520 lower 0.0074. These point estimates are above benchmark and the intervals exclude zero, but every lower bound is below 0.0415, so they do not establish a clinically meaningful association at the pre-specified uncertainty standard. Because these features have low prevalence, the complement strata are large and therefore mechanically related to the main effect; they should not be treated as multiple independent confirmations.\n\nThe feature_042=1 strata within these same splits are weak, null, or opposite: H210 0.0292 with wide interval; H212 0.0367 with interval crossing zero; H216 0.0080; H218 0.0009; H220 0.0108. This reduces confidence that the complement positives represent meaningful biological subgroup effects. I reject H213 and H214 as clinically meaningful claimed associations because their signed intervals exclude benchmark values in the claimed direction: H213 estimate -0.0073 upper 0.0150, and H214 estimate -0.0495 upper 0.0377, both with upper bounds below 0.0415. This does not prove absence of any statistical effect, only that these specific strata do not support a clinically meaningful stronger-dependency claim. H210, H212, H216, H218, and H220 remain unresolved but closed as coherence tests because their intervals still include clinically meaningful values but provide no positive evidence for acceptance.\n\nThe central H19 main effect remains unresolved: signed estimate 0.0440 with interval 0.0035 to 0.0844. The point estimate is near benchmark, but the lower bound is far below it. The new coherence results do not close H19: they suggest that the signal may be distributed across large complements, but they also reveal heterogeneity and do not reach interval-level clinical support. The most informative validation target is therefore H19 itself, not a subgroup selected after seeing the data. I request validation of H19. If independent validation raises the lower bound to at least about 0.0415, H19 can be accepted as a dataset-specific clinically meaningful association. If validation does not, the feature_042-outcome_001 line should be closed as unsupported, even if some exploratory complement strata have positive point estimates. \n\nMajor limitations: multiplicity from many predictor-outcome comparisons, lack of adjustment for correlated predictors, rare strata with unstable estimates, high outcome variance for outcome_001, and the fact that all subgroup splits were selected after inspecting related contrasts. These are associations in this dataset only, not causal or generalizable effects.",
